// Summary statistics over the innermost axis, as Blade source.
//
// These are the one-line reductions that every tutorial and worked example
// re-declares by hand (`docs/quickstart-1.md`, `examples/01_weather_stations`,
// CLAUDE.md's style table all open with the same `mean`). They are not
// intrinsics and deliberately are not: each is a two-token composition of
// `reduce` / `extents`, so the stdlib is the right home and the compiler is
// not the right home. Nothing here is privileged -- read the source, it is
// exactly what you would have written.
//
//     from stats import mean
//     let m = mean([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])       // 2.0
//
//     import stats
//     let m = stats.mean([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]) // same, qualified
//
// RANK. Every function takes `T^1` and returns `T^0`: one row in, one scalar
// out. That is what makes them usable as KERNELS -- `method_for(A) <@> mean`
// applies `mean` per row of a rank-2 `A` and deduces the rank-1 result, and
// `(a - mean(a)) * (b - mean(b))` inside a `where comm(a, b)` kernel is the
// centering step of a covariance. A whole-array fold is `reduce(A, (+),
// axes = k)`, not one of these.
//
// UNITS ride through all three, and this is checked, not hoped for: `mean` is a
// sum divided by a COUNT, so its result carries the row's unit
// (`Float64<meters>` row -> `Float64<meters>`), `variance` squares it
// (-> `Float64<meters^2>`), and `stddev` halves that back to
// `Float64<meters>`. None of them needs a unit annotation to say so; the unit
// algebra derives the exponent from the body once per declaration and applies
// it to whatever the caller passes.
//
// Until 2026-08-15 none of that was enforced at a CALL. A generic signature's
// deduced `T^0` return reached the caller with no signature at all -- direct
// application never unifies parameters against arguments, so the caller's unit
// never entered the substitution -- and `mean(x) + Float<seconds>` was accepted
// over a `Float<meters>` row. `stddev` carried a UNIT WARNING here blaming
// `sqrt`; the blame was misplaced (its input was blank, not its rule) and the
// warning is gone. Pins: tests/corpus/units/077 and
// tests/corpus/unit-errors/019-021.
//
// NO SAMPLE (n-1) VARIANTS, on purpose. `variance` and `stddev` below are the
// POPULATION forms, dividing by n. A `sample_variance` dividing by n-1 would
// be a second name one character away from the first whose only difference is
// invisible at the call site, and picking the wrong one is a silent numerical
// bug, not a type error. Divide by `extents(row) - 1` explicitly when you want
// Bessel's correction, so the choice is written down where it is made.
module stats

/// Arithmetic mean of a row. `mean(row) = (sum of row) / (length of row)`.
function mean(row: T^1) -> T^0 = reduce(row, (+)) / extents(row)

/// Population variance of a row: the mean squared deviation from the mean.
/// Divides by n, not n-1 (see the module note). Written as one centering pass
/// plus one fold rather than as `mean((row - mean(row)) ^ 2)` so it does not
/// depend on cross-module monomorphization of a generic calling a generic.
function variance(row: T^1) -> T^0 = {
    let n = extents(row)
    let m = reduce(row, (+)) / n
    let centered = row - m
    reduce(centered * centered, (+)) / n
}

/// Population standard deviation: the square root of `variance`.
///
/// UNITS: `sqrt` halves even exponents, so a `Float64<meters>` row gives
/// `Float64<meters>` -- and that IS checked at the call site now (see the
/// module note). An `Float64<meters^2>` annotation on this result is a BL3006.
function stddev(row: T^1) -> T^0 = sqrt(variance(row))
